2:T59a,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What makes a high-converting landing page headline?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A high-converting headline states the specific outcome the buyer wants, not a description of the product. It answers 'what's in it for me?' in under 8 words. The best headlines name the problem being solved or the result being delivered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I test if my headline is weak?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Cover your logo and product name. Read only the headline. Would a cold visitor know what they get, who it's for, and why it matters? If the answer is no to any of these, the headline is weak. Free brief audit scores your headline across 9 conversion signals."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the money angle for a headline?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The money angle connects the headline directly to financial loss or gain - 'Stop burning ad budget on a page that can't convert.' It works best for cold paid-traffic visitors because it matches the pain they're actively feeling."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long should a landing page headline be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"6–12 words is the sweet spot. Longer than 12 words loses punch. Under 6 words often lacks specificity. The goal is one clear result, stated as concisely as possible."}}]}c:X
