4:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-14","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-fg mb-6","children":"Six headline failures that drive visitors away"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-start gap-3 mb-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 font-mono text-xs text-fg-dim mt-0.5","children":"01"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent mb-0.5","children":"Headline (H1)"}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-base font-semibold text-fg","children":"Product name as H1 - 'Blue Widget Pro' states what it is, not what it does"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6 mb-3 pl-7","children":"Using the product name as the primary headline is the most common H1 mistake on B2B SaaS and direct-response pages. The product name carries zero meaning for a cold visitor who has never heard of it. 'Blue Widget Pro' tells them nothing about who it is for, what problem it solves, or what changes after they sign up. The product name belongs in the title tag and brand mark - not in the highest-weight copy on the page."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"pl-7 border-l-2 border-accent/30 ml-7","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs font-semibold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider mb-1","children":"Fix"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"Replace the product name with the buyer's outcome or situation. One test: remove the product name from the H1 and ask whether a cold visitor would now understand what they get. If the answer is yes, the new headline is better. The product name can live in the logo, the nav, and the title tag."}]]}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-start gap-3 mb-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 font-mono text-xs text-fg-dim mt-0.5","children":"02"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-accent mb-0.5","children":"Specificity"}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-base font-semibold text-fg","children":"Category description instead of outcome - 'All-in-one platform' vs 'Track all your team's tasks in one place'"}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6 mb-3 pl-7","children":"'All-in-one platform', 'Collaborative workspace', 'The modern solution for teams' - these describe a product category, not a result. A cold visitor evaluating your page already knows what category they are looking at. What they don't know is whether your product solves their specific problem. Category headlines fail because they force the visitor to do the translation work: 'OK, it's a platform - but what does it actually do for me?' Most don't bother."}],["$","div",null,{"className":"pl-7 border-l-2 border-accent/30 ml-7","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xs font-semibold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider mb-1","children":"Fix"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"State what the visitor achieves, not what the product category is. 'Track all your team's tasks in one place' is specific. 'All-in-one platform' is not. The test: can you swap your headline onto a competitor's page without changing anything? If yes, the headline is too generic."}]]}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-start gap-3 mb-3","children":["$L15","$L16"]}],"$L17","$L18"]}],"$L19","$L1a","$L1b"]}]]}]
